    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis investigates the role of political campaigns in state-building and regime consolidation in Maoist China, focusing on the Resist America and Aid Korea (RAAK) Campaign. It addresses the central question of why top-down policy implementation in a highly centralized system results in different outcomes across regions. The study posits that these variations are influenced by the strategic selectiveness of policy implementation, based on the the combination of structural factors like regional economic and social conditions and the agency of local officials who adapt central directives to local contexts. Using the RAAK Campaign as a case study, this research explores how political campaigns mobilized resources, suppressed potential opposition, and tested obedience. The analysis shows that regions with weaker Communist control, such as Guangdong and Jiangxi, experienced more intense and early mobilization efforts compared to more securely controlled regions like Shanxi. The findings are based on a dataset from provincial and county-level local chronicles and archival materials, using Propensity Score Matching (PSM) to ensure validity. This thesis contributes to the understanding of state-society interactions in authoritarian settings, highlighting the strategic use of political campaigns for regime consolidation and state-building in early years of the People’s Republic of China.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
